e allOrders fold record contradictions (negative executedQty, explicit 0 beside a positive amount) instead of zeros, that an allOrders-terminal request resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set and pin, and that the ledger reader owns the row's columns at emission (a branch cannot clear them). Round 8 (review 82cbe018): that the submit response and the amount re-query are MERGED per field (a record lacking executedQty never revokes an earlier 20; an explicit terminal 0 read at the GET entry is a measured zero; a falling executedQty or NEW after terminal is a contradiction), that an allOrders match resolves the k-cancel's unresolved set only with an explicit terminal status AND our identity (no status ⇒ exact query; origQty/symbol/side mismatch ⇒ UNKNOWN), that a fold contradiction makes the name UNKNOWN end to end (no top-up, reader refuses), and that with a ledger the row's amount and price are the reader's (an unclosed ledger writes None and relabels). It does NOT run phase C or the watchdog itself (reconcile is called on the emitted rows), its venue is FakeNet. Round 9 (review b92e9479, DESIGN §3c): that a derived quantity or amount comes from ONE record (an amount 40 in one snapshot and a price 3 in another derive nothing — C unknown, 40 a lower bound, the row a band), that a terminal executedQty is CONSTANT (20 then 30 is a contradiction end to end; a terminal record without a C may still learn it; child fills beyond a terminal C are a contradiction), that the k-cancel's DELETE body is a record of the request (CANCELED 4 survives a sparse terminal query ⇒ top-up 6; a differing terminal 6 ⇒ contradiction, no top-up; an unreached symbol's UNKNOWN row carries the 4; -2013 after a cancel with facts is a contradiction, not absent), that a sparse terminal record (no executedQty, no cumQuote) is unreadable rather than 0, that the identity gate governs FACTS (a record failing it makes the request unmeasurable instead of entering the ledger as C 4 / terminal; a missing side, a non-finite origQty and an orderId other than the acknowledged one fail it; a side-less fill is never read as SELL), that contradictions travel with the facts on the partial path (query and page agree), and that the amount re-query's record must be OUR request (client id / symbol / orderId / origQty / side compared when the record carries them; a mismatch makes the request unmeasurable). Round 10 (review 38808cbd, DESIGN §3d): that a quantity carries its COMPLETENESS TYPE (an open snapshot's 20 followed by a terminal record without a quantity is a lower bound — [20, Q], known 70 / band 30 beside a second leg of 50, legal 70–100 CLEAN, 60 and 110 anomalies — while a terminal snapshot's 20 is final; a child-fill subset raises the lower bound and never proves the total; a child set that reaches Q does), that every UNKNOWN exit first folds the records already held (a CANCELED 4 survives an exhausted or -2013 query — the latter as a contradiction; a settlement cancel that answered CANCELED 6 survives a failed re-query; the submit response's own PARTIALLY_FILLED 4 is the first record; a status-only DELETE body proves existence and terminality, so -2013 after it is a contradiction, not absent), and that two final snapshots with different amounts contradict. Round 11 (review f79dc8cd, DESIGN §3e): that a final amount is never divided by a lower-bound quantity to make a price, that a maker leg the venue told us about carries a request ledger so its quantity is the reader's band (open 4 / final amount 6 ⇒ known 4 / band 6, total [8, 14] beside a top-up of 4), that a non-object query answer still folds the records already held, that a child subset's amount is a lower bound (the amount closes only with the set; reconcile reads a ledger row's band whether or not its amount closed), that a missing or None finality key is NOT final (no legacy fallback) while a lower bound reaching Q is final by capacity, that a top-up response without a status proves no finality, and that the submit record is bound by the client id WE sent (a response naming another id lends no orderId and fails the present-field gate). Round 12 (review 83fcbbcc, DESIGN §3f / §5 field-by-reader table): that the submit response's direct reader checks the body against the order we sent before reading a number (a body naming another client id is a contradiction, and no re-query is made under that id), that a merged record whose quantity 0 is a LOWER BOUND is compatible with a positive final amount (NEW 0 → fills → cancel with an amount-only terminal read ⇒ known 0 / band 10 / amount 4, total [6, 16] beside a top-up of 6) while a raw same-snapshot 0-beside-amount stays a contradiction, that a final amount is constant against child fills and a subset's amount may not exceed it, that a duplicate trade id without a quote never erases a recorded quote, that a child subset's vwap goes to its own column and never becomes the row's price, that an amount known beside an unknown quantity is the interval [0, Q] (known 0 + band, on the UNKNOWN row and the normal maker ledger alike — a contract choice registered in DESIGN §3f.6), and that explicit zeros carry the amount's finality. Round 13 (review ec2fe835, DESIGN §3g): that the child-fill amount LOWER BOUND (Σ over the children that carry a quote) is compared against a final amount without waiting for the others (5/N50 + 5/N-missing against a final 40 is a contradiction), that a non-finite ledger amount is flagged even when the quantity is missing, that every identity gate reads a present-but-malformed field (empty side, non-finite origQty) as a contradiction and an absent one as no evidence (one `identity_field` for the submit gate, the re-query gate and the settlement's present-field gate), and that the two cost diagnostics (neutrality_price, chase_readout.collect) measure bps over PRICED fills only and report the unpriced count / notional / fee beside it (no priced fill ⇒ None, never 0). Round 14 (review d51cb731): that an order id